(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old has a rough lesion on the back of his hand with a cauliflower-like surface and small black dots at the centre. The skin lines stop at its edge. Squeezing it from the sides hurts more than pressing on it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Callus","Cutaneous horn","Verruca vulgaris","Clavus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — interrupted skin lines, blackened centre, and pain on side pressure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"The parents ask what the lesion will do as their son grows. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will grow with him, darken and thicken, and will not involute","It will proliferate for a few months and then slowly involute","It will fade within his first year of life","It will stay exactly the same size and colour for life"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the vasculature dilates over time and the lesion may become a raised plaque. Psychosocial disability from facial disfigurement can be overwhelming. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A patient with neurofibromatosis type 1 attends for routine review. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["A cutaneous examination at every visit","Excision of every cutaneous neurofibroma as it appears","Annual magnetic resonance imaging of the whole body","Intralesional corticosteroid to any enlarging lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — assess for new neurofibromas or progression of existing ones. Plexiform lesions may be locally invasive, so their extent needs determining. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm brown nodule on her lower leg. She thinks it appeared after an insect bite two years ago. Squeezing it laterally makes it retract beneath the surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keloid","Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma","Cutaneous melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the dimple sign, on the commonest site, after a bite. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Six weeks later the cyst is quiet and the patient wants it gone permanently. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Surgical removal of the entire capsule","Aspiration of the contents with a wide-bore needle","Repeated intralesional steroid until it disappears","Cryotherapy to the overlying skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a small cyst of one to three centimetres can instead be punched and emptied. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has a soft, painless, rubbery subcutaneous nodule about 4 cm across on his back. It is mobile and there is no overlying pore. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Abscess","Lipoma","Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the most common soft tissue tumour, soft and rubbery, usually under five centimetres. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A patient with a pyogenic granuloma opts against treatment. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["It is premalignant and must be excised within a month","It is infectious and household contacts should be checked","It will resolve within two weeks without any follow-up","It is benign and often resolves over months to years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — effective treatments exist if she changes her mind. If it recurs, early follow-up is appropriate, because small lesions are easier to treat than large ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 29-year-old asks how to stop her calluses coming back. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Well-fitting shoes and socks, and pads inside the shoe","Apply sunscreen daily and examine the skin periodically","Avoid ear piercing and other cosmetic procedures","Reposition every two hours and keep the skin clean and dry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — remove the friction and the lesion stops re-forming. Tight shoes and high heels are named as causes, as is wearing shoes without socks. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 41-year-old runner has a tender lesion in the web space between the fourth and fifth toes. It is whitish and boggy rather than hard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hard corn","Callus","Plantar wart","Soft corn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — clavus mollum, softened by moisture trapped between the toes. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The same patient asks about having them removed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions will resolve on their own within a year","Removal is curative and recurrence does not occur","Treatment is for cosmesis only, and every option has a trade-off","The lesions must be removed because they can become malignant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — drugs such as oral isotretinoin carry an increased risk of recurrence, and removal procedures carry a risk of poor cosmetic results. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Dermoscopy is performed on that patient's leg lesion. Which finding would support the diagnosis?","choices":["A moth-eaten border with uniform pigment throughout","An epithelial collarette at the base of the lesion","A central keratin-filled crater within a smooth dome","A peripheral pigment network with a central white mass"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the white centre corresponds to the fibrous core. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"That patient finds the nail deformity troublesome. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Observation, since the cyst is asymptomatic","Repeated aspiration at each visit","Intralesional corticosteroid followed by silicone sheeting","Excision, since nail dystrophy is an indication"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — asymptomatic lesions can simply be observed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman admitted after a hip fracture has a heel wound with full-thickness skin loss and yellow adipose tissue clearly visible in the base. No tendon or bone can be seen. Which stage is this?","choices":["Unstageable","Stage 3","Stage 4","Stage 2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — full thickness with fat visible, and nothing deeper exposed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man on immunosuppression after a renal transplant has several soft, whitish-yellow papules on his forehead. Each is 3 to 4 mm with a central dell, and a tiny globule of sebum can be expressed. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma","Syringoma","Xanthelasma","Sebaceous hyperplasia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — immunosuppression is named as high risk, and the central umbilication is characteristic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 62-year-old attends about a new lesion on his sun-exposed forearm. Beyond addressing the lesion, which counselling does the lecture say to offer?","choices":["Well-fitting footwear and padding inside the shoe","Sunscreen, sun avoidance at peak hours, and skin checks","Avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","Repositioning every two hours and barrier creams"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to any new lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has several small vascular lesions with a central punctum and radiating vessels across his upper chest and arms. Which history is most important to take?","choices":["Alcohol use, liver-damaging medications, and hormone use","Footwear, occupation and use of hand tools","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status","Birth history, feeding and family history of birthmarks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck also says to ask about pregnancies. Spider angiomas are associated with estrogen excess and with cirrhosis and liver failure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"The patient asks whether you can simply watch that lesion, since he has read they go away. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Observe for three to six months, since regression is the natural history","Take a dermoscopy image and review in three months","Apply a topical keratolytic to flatten the crater","Excise or destroy it, because of possible malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — strong arguments support classifying keratoacanthoma as a variant of invasive squamous cell carcinoma, so the standard of care is not observation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who wears narrow dress shoes has a painful lesion on the dorsal aspect of her fifth toe. It is under a centimetre, sharply demarcated, and the skin lines run through it. Pressing straight down on it reproduces her pain. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hard corn","Verruca vulgaris","Callus","Soft corn"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a well-defined lesion, tender on direct pressure, with skin lines running through. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The patient asks whether his lesion is a sebaceous cyst full of oil. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It is mucin extruded from a nearby joint","The contents are keratin, not sebum","Yes, it is a blocked sebaceous gland filled with sebum","It is a collection of lipid-laden macrophages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is often called a sebaceous cyst because the material looks like sebum, but the deck states outright that it is not one. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The same mother asks whether the lesion will need surgery. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will fade within her first year without any treatment","It will regress over three to six months after a period of stability","Most involute on their own over several years","It will grow with her throughout life and will not involute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — complete involution in fifty per cent by age five, seventy per cent by seven and ninety per cent by nine, so serial observation is often all that is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A patient asks whether she can remove a skin tag herself with nail scissors. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["She should first apply a keratolytic for two weeks to soften it","She should not, because skin tags are premalignant","She should not, because skin tags bleed when they come off","She may, provided she sterilises the scissors first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and a new tag often forms in the same area anyway. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A patient's keloid has been treated with laser. Which addition does the lecture say gives the best result?","choices":["Combining the laser with over-the-counter keratolytics","Combining the laser with oral antibiotics","Combining the laser with beta-blockade","Combining the laser with intralesional steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — laser works by shrinking collagen or inducing microvascular thrombosis. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"That patient wants the lesion removed and asks which option is least likely to come back. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Observation, since spontaneous resolution is guaranteed within weeks","Surgical excision, though it scars the most","Cryotherapy, which has the lowest recurrence and no scarring","Laser, which has the lowest recurrence and the best cosmetic result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it has the lowest recurrence rate and also provides histopathologic analysis, at the cost of the highest rate of scarring. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is to have a keratoacanthoma excised from her forearm. Which margin does the lecture specify?","choices":["Five millimetres","One millimetre","Ten millimetres","Two centimetres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with Mohs surgery for large, recurrent or cosmetically sensitive lesions. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on both lower eyelids and upper cheeks. They are asymptomatic and appeared around puberty. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Syringoma","Xanthelasma","Milia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — benign eccrine duct neoplasms, appearing at puberty, females more than males. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 52-year-old carpenter has thickened, poorly demarcated, painless skin across both palms. The skin lines run through the thickened area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Keratoacanthoma","Callus","Hard corn"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — broad-area pressure, diffuse thickening, no central core, painless. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 26-year-old with a keloid on the sternum wants it removed. Which is the most appropriate advice about surgical excision alone?","choices":["Recurrence is fifty to one hundred per cent, so steroid injection follows","Recurrence is under five per cent, so excision alone is usually curative","Excision is contraindicated in keloids because it always causes bleeding","Excision is preferred over all other options because it removes the fibrous tissue completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the recurrent lesion is often larger than the original, which is why excision is never done alone. Combination therapy has the best success rates. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The same patient asks whether removing them will stop new ones appearing. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They will fade on their own within a year","Each one should be biopsied because they are premalignant","New lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them","Removing them prevents further lesions from forming"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary unless they bother him. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"The same patient's abscess is tense and fluctuant. Which is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":["Laser hair removal to the natal cleft","Incision and drainage","Oral antibiotics alone, with review in one week","Referral to a surgeon for definitive excision now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an acute abscess requires drainage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has soft, yellow plaques on both medial upper eyelids. They are asymptomatic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Xanthelasma","Syringoma","Milia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — collections of lipid-laden macrophages, most commonly on the medial eyelids. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"}]};
